<div class="wrap">

  <header>
    <div>
      <div class="eyebrow">College Majors · AI Exposure</div>
      <h1>Which college majors sit closest to what AI can already do?</h1>
      <div class="sub">Every rectangle is a bachelor's degree field. Size = degrees conferred per year — the
        latest NCES-finalized figures (2021–22). Color = an estimated exposure score, 0–10, for how much of the
        field's typical day-to-day task content overlaps with current generative AI capability. Category headers
        carry a ▲/▼ where fresh 2025–26 enrollment data points to where sizes are actually heading next.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="credit">Format inspired by Andrej Karpathy's<br>
      <a href="https://github.com/karpathy/jobs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AI exposure treemap of BLS occupations</a><br>
      — applied here to U.S. college majors.</div>
  </header>

  <div class="stats">
    <div class="stat"><div class="n" id="statMajors">–</div><div class="l">Majors shown</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="n" id="statDegrees">–</div><div class="l">Degrees / year</div></div>
    <div class="stat accent"><div class="n" id="statAvg">–</div><div class="l">Avg. exposure (wtd.)</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="n" id="statHigh">–</div><div class="l">Most exposed, shown</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="n" id="statLow">–</div><div class="l">Least exposed, shown</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="data-note">
    <b>Why the sizes still say 2021–22:</b> NCES's detailed major-by-major completions table (Digest Table 322.10)
    has a structural ~4-year publication lag — 2021–22 is still the newest finalized breakdown as of August 2026;
    2023–24 exists only at the provisional, un-detailed level. But the freshest real signal — <b>Spring 2026
    enrollment</b> — shows a sharp inflection already underway: Computer &amp; Information Sciences enrollment
    fell 8.4% at four-year schools (9.3–11.2% elsewhere) for a second straight term, its lowest persistence rate
    of any top-10 major, while Health Professions grew 6–7% for a third straight year and Engineering also rose.
    That's shown as ▲/▼ badges on the category headers below — a leading indicator of where the rectangles are
    headed, not yet baked into their size. <span class="src">Sources: NCES Digest 2023, Table 322.10 · National
    Student Clearinghouse Research Center, Final Fall/Spring Enrollment Trends &amp; 2026 Persistence and
    Retention Report.</span>
  </div>

  <div class="data-note">
    <b>Exposure scoring — now a real crosswalk.</b> Every major below (bar two) is mapped to its actual closest
    occupation(s) in Eloundou, Manning, Mishkin &amp; Rock's "GPTs are GPTs" dataset (OpenAI/UPenn, 2023) — the
    real published exposure scores, not my own estimates. Score = the average of their human-rated and GPT-4-rated
    "β" measure (share of the occupation's tasks meaningfully exposed), scaled to 0–10. Majors marked
    <span class="prov-cited">● cited</span> — <b>89 of 91</b> — link straight to the occupation(s) their graduates
    actually go on to do; hover any rectangle for the exact SOC code and both scores. Only <i>Liberal Arts &amp;
    General Studies</i> and <i>Interdisciplinary &amp; General Studies</i> stay as rubric estimates — genuinely too
    heterogeneous for one occupation to represent. A few real numbers reshuffled the picture: <b>Animal
    Science jumps to 7.0</b> (its closest match, Animal Scientists, was human-rated surprisingly exposed — mostly
    research and reporting work, not just hands-on husbandry), while <b>Fire Science and Fitness &amp; Wellness
    drop to 1.0</b>, among the lowest scores on the board. I also layered in real <b>Anthropic Economic Index</b>
    figures (actual Claude usage, not hypothetical ratings): as of Feb 2026, <b>49%</b> of U.S. jobs have had
    Claude used for at least a quarter of their tasks (up from 36% a year earlier), and augmented use (52%) still
    edges out fully automated use (45%). One caveat worth carrying into Computer Science's score below: Anthropic's
    own data shows software developers are <i>less</i> affected than naive task-coverage implies, once weighted by
    how hard the delegated tasks actually are. <span class="src">Sources: Eloundou, Manning, Mishkin &amp; Rock,
    "GPTs are GPTs" (arXiv:2303.10130 / Science 2024), occ_level.csv, github.com/openai/GPTs-are-GPTs · Anthropic
    Economic Index reports, Jan &amp; Mar 2026.</span>
  </div>

  <div class="controls">
    <div class="search"><input type="text" id="searchInput" placeholder="Search a major…"></div>
    <select class="catfilter" id="catFilter"></select>
    <div class="range-group">Exposure ≥ <input type="range" id="minExp" min="0" max="10" step="0.5" value="0"><span class="val" id="minExpVal">0</span></div>
    <div class="range-group">≤ <input type="range" id="maxExp" min="0" max="10" step="0.5" value="10"><span class="val" id="maxExpVal">10</span></div>
    <div class="seg" id="colorSeg">
      <button data-mode="exposure" class="active">Color: Exposure</button>
      <button data-mode="category">Color: Field</button>
    </div>
    <button class="reset" id="resetBtn">Reset filters</button>
  </div>

  <div class="legend-row">
    <div class="exp-legend" id="expLegend"><span>Low exposure</span><div class="exp-gradient"></div><span>High exposure</span>
      <span style="margin-left:14px;display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:5px;"><span style="width:7px;height:7px;border-radius:50%;background:#3C6E4A;display:inline-block;"></span> = cited vs. Eloundou et al. real occupation data — hover for detail</span>
    </div>
    <div class="cat-legend" id="catLegend" style="display:none;"></div>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-shell"><svg id="chart"></svg></div>

  <div id="detail"><button class="close-x" id="closeDetail">✕</button></div>

  <footer>
    <b>Methodology.</b> Degree counts: NCES Digest of Education Statistics / IPEDS, newest finalized detailed
    breakdown (2021–22, ~2.0M bachelor's degrees), broad categories split into majors using typical sub-field
    proportions. Category ▲/▼ badges: real Spring 2026 enrollment momentum from the National Student
    Clearinghouse — a separate, faster-moving signal layered on top, not baked into the sizes. Exposure scores:
    <span class="prov-cited">● cited</span> majors (89 of 91) are mapped to their actual closest occupation(s) —
    typically the field graduates most commonly enter — and scored using Eloundou, Manning, Mishkin &amp; Rock's
    "GPTs are GPTs" (arXiv:2303.10130), the real published occupation-level dataset, averaging their human-rated
    and GPT-4-rated β measure and scaling to 0–10. The occupation-to-major mapping itself (which SOC code best
    represents each major) is my own judgment, built on standard career-pathway knowledge, not an official
    crosswalk — a different reasonable mapping would shift some scores. Only Liberal Arts &amp; General Studies
    and Interdisciplinary &amp; General Studies remain rubric estimates, honestly uncited, since no single
    occupation reasonably represents them. Anthropic Economic Index figures (real Claude.ai usage data, not
    hypothetical ratings) are layered in as separate context above. Treat the whole thing as an illustrative
    exploration, not a forecast of which majors or careers will change — the same caveat Karpathy gave his own
    project.
  </footer>
</div>

